<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petrobey Mavromichalis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Petrobey, Petros Mavromichalis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petros Mavromichalis, known as Petrobey, was the last bey of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> peninsula under Ottoman suzerainty. In March 1821 he raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a> at the start of the Greek War of Independence and led the Messenian Senate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a>. He later opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a>, whose imprisonment of him led his kinsmen to assassinate the governor in 1831.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrobey-mavromichalis.jpg" alt="Petrobey Mavromichalis" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodoros Vryzakis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%92%CF%81%CF%85%CE%B6%CE%AC%CE%BA%CE%B7%CF%82,_%CE%98%CE%B5%CF%8C%CE%B4%CF%89%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_-_%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%84%CF%81%CF%8C%CE%BC%CF%80%CE%B5%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%9C%CE%B1%CF%85%CF%81%CE%BF%CE%BC%CE%B9%CF%87%CE%AC%CE%BB%CE%B7%CF%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Petros was born on 6 August 1765, the son of Pierros Mavromichalis Pierrakos and Katerina Koutsogrigorakos, a doctor's daughter. His family had a long record of risings against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>: his grandfather Georgakis and his father were among the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orlov-revolt">Orlov Revolt</a>. In the feuding among the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> that followed, the young Petros won a reputation for settling disputes and reconciling the warring families.</p><p>After failed uprisings he helped many klephts and other rebels escape to the French-controlled Heptanese, which gave him useful contacts, and he may have reached an understanding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, then fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, for a Greek revolt timed with a French blow against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Napoleon's failure in Egypt ended the plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1814 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a> were again a threat, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> made concessions that included naming him Bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, formalising the autonomy the region already had. Under Petrobey, as he was now called, the Maniots kept the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> against Albanian raiders on the Sultan's behalf, while he took part in the plans of the Moreot captains for a rising. He joined the Filiki Eteria in 1818 and in 1819 brokered a pact among the leading captains' families.</p><p>On 17 March 1821 he raised his war flag at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopolis">Areopolis</a>, and his men took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a> on 23 March. After the summer of 1822 he left the fighting to his sons, two of whom were killed, and went on mediating quarrels among the captains and writing to leaders and philhellenes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Petrobey sat in the first Greek Senate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a>, but the two clashed over Kapodistrias's plan for a centralised administration of appointed officials in place of the old loyalties of family. When his brother Ioannis rose against the appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Lakonia</a>, both brothers were arrested on coming to negotiate. After the assassination by his brother Konstantinos and his son Georgios, which he publicly disapproved of, he served under King Otto as vice-president of the Council of State and later as a senator, and received the Grand Cross of the Order of the Redeemer. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> on 17 January 1848 and was buried with the highest honours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petrobey Mavromichalis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Notable beys included Tzanetos Grigorakis and, from 1815, <b>Petros Mavromichalis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-mani">Beylik of Mani</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Greek revolt broke out in March 1821, <b>Petros Mavromichalis</b> formed the Messenian Senate at Kalamata.</blockquote><a href="/state/peloponnesian-senate">Peloponnesian Senate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Areopoli, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.67,22.38&amp;year=1818" aria-label="Areopoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Petrobey Mavromichalis"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4479 4414h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4479 4414h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.67,22.38&amp;year=1818">What was here in 1818? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
